.TH QSH_CONFIG 5 "" "qsh" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
qsh_config \- qsh configuration files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qsh/config
.RI ( ~/.config/qsh/config )
.br
.I /etc/qsh/qsh_config
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR qsh (1)
and
.BR qsh\-server (1)
need no configuration. These files only change their defaults: the user's file first,
then the system's. Neither has to exist.
.PP
Everything about reaching a host comes from
.BR ssh (1)
and its own configuration
.RI ( ~/.ssh/config ,
.BR ssh_config (5)):
host aliases, users, ports, keys, agents, jump hosts and known hosts work for
.B qsh
exactly as for
.BR ssh .
These files hold only what is particular to qsh.
.PP
The files are TOML (https://toml.io), with three kinds of table:
.TP
.BI [host." pattern "]
Settings for the hosts that match
.I pattern
(see
.BR PATTERNS ).
A file may have any number of them.
.TP
.B [defaults]
Settings for every host.
.TP
.B [server]
Settings of the
.BR qsh\-server (1)
daemon on the machine whose file this is (see
.BR "SERVER SETTINGS" ).
.SH PRECEDENCE
For each setting, the first value found is used, as in
.BR ssh_config (5):
the user's file is read before the system's, and in each file the matching
.B [host]
tables are read in the order they appear, then
.BR [defaults] .
Put specific tables before general ones. A setting in the user's
.B [defaults]
thus wins over one in a
.B [host]
table of the system's file.
.PP
Environment variables (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT )
override both files, and command line options override everything:
.PP
.RS
command line > environment > user's file > system's file > built-in default
.RE
.SH PATTERNS
The name of a
.B [host]
table is a list of patterns separated by commas or spaces. In a pattern,
.B *
matches any run of characters and
.B ?
exactly one; letters match regardless of case. A pattern that starts with
.B !
is negated.
.PP
The table applies to a connection when one of its patterns matches the host as typed on the
command line (without the user name and port) or the host name that
.BR ssh (1)
resolves it to (its
.B HostName
setting), and no negated pattern matches either of them. A list of negated patterns alone
matches nothing.
.PP
.RS
.nf
[host."web*"]                      # web, web1, WEB\-db
[host."*.example.com,!*.lab.example.com"]
[host."jump? gateway"]             # jump1, jumpx, gateway
.fi
.RE
.SH CLIENT SETTINGS
These may appear in
.B [host]
tables and in
.BR [defaults] .
.TP
.B transports
The transports to try, in order of preference, of \fB"quic"\fR (QUIC over UDP),
\fB"tls"\fR (TLS over TCP) and \fB"ssh"\fR (a pipe through
.BR ssh (1)).
They are raced: the first starts at once, each next one 400 ms after the one before, but the
ssh pipe, which costs an ssh login, not before 3 seconds unless it comes first. Transports
not listed are not used. Default: \fB["quic", "tls", "ssh"]\fR.
.TP
.B server_command
The path of
.B qsh\-server
on the host, for installations outside the search path. Default: search
.B PATH
and
.IR ~/.local/bin .
.TP
.B escape_char
The escape character (see
.BR qsh (1)):
one character, \fB"^\fIX\fB"\fR for a control character (\fB"^]"\fR), or \fB"none"\fR to
turn escapes off. Default: \fB"~"\fR.
.TP
.B predict
Predictive local echo: \fB"auto"\fR, \fB"always"\fR or \fB"never"\fR. Reserved: this
version never predicts. Default: \fB"auto"\fR.
.TP
.B status_line
Show a status line while the connection is lost: \fBtrue\fR or \fBfalse\fR. Default:
\fBtrue\fR.
.TP
.B ssh
The ssh program to run. Default: \fBssh\fR, from
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B ssh_options
Options for
.BR ssh (1),
an array of strings such as \fB["\-o", "ServerAliveInterval=30"]\fR. They are given after
the options of the command line, so those win (ssh uses the first value it gets). Default:
none.
.TP
.B keepalive
How often to send something on an idle connection, so that NAT routers keep it open: a time
(see
.BR "TIME AND SIZE FORMATS" )
of 1 second to 1 hour, or \fB"auto"\fR for qsh's own interval. Default: \fB"auto"\fR.
.TP
.B install
What to do when the host has no
.BR qsh\-server :
\fB"ask"\fR offers once, on a terminal, to install it; \fB"never"\fR exits with status 42.
Default: \fB"ask"\fR.
.TP
.B replay_on_attach
When a new client attaches to a running session, show the output the server kept for it:
\fBtrue\fR or \fBfalse\fR. Default: \fBtrue\fR.
.SH SERVER SETTINGS
These go in the
.B [server]
table and its
.B [server.preauth]
table. They are read by
.BR qsh\-server (1)
from the files of the user it runs as (each user has a daemon of their own), the user's file
again before the system's.
.TP
.B ports
The ports the daemon may listen on: it uses the first one free on both UDP and TCP, so that
every user of a shared host gets one. \fB"\fIFIRST\fB\-\fILAST\fB"\fR or a port number.
Default: \fB"60443\-60542"\fR.
.TP
.B extra_ports
More ports to listen on, for networks that block the first, as an array of port numbers.
Reserved: this version does not use it.
.TP
.B max_sessions
The most sessions one daemon keeps, from 1 to 100000. Default: 1000.
.TP
.B detached_ttl
How long a session nobody is attached to is kept (its programs then get SIGHUP), a time of
at most a year. Default: \fB"6h"\fR.
.TP
.B exited_ttl
How long a session whose program exited is kept for its client to collect the output, a
time of at most a year. Default: \fB"1h"\fR.
.TP
.B replay_bytes
How much output each session keeps for clients that come back, a size from 1 MiB to 1 GiB.
Default: \fB"8M"\fR.
.PP
.B [server.preauth]
limits the connections that have not authenticated yet:
.TP
.B connections
Unauthenticated connections per daemon, from 1 to 65536. Default: 64.
.TP
.B per_source
Unauthenticated connections per source address (IPv6: per /64), from 1 to 65536. Default: 8.
.TP
.B failure_burst
Failed authentications a source may cause in a burst before it is refused for a while, from
1 to 10000. Default: 10.
.TP
.B failure_refill
After a burst, one more failure is allowed per this much time, from 1 second to a day.
Default: \fB"6s"\fR.
.SH TIME AND SIZE FORMATS
A time is a number of seconds, or a string of numbers each followed by a unit,
.B s
(seconds),
.B m
(minutes),
.B h
(hours),
.B d
(days) or
.B w
(weeks), which are added up: \fB30\fR, \fB"90s"\fR, \fB"10m"\fR, \fB"1h30m"\fR.
.PP
A size is a number of bytes, or a string of a number followed by
.BR K ,
.B M
or
.B G
(powers of 1024; \fBKiB\fR, \fBMiB\fR and \fBGiB\fR mean the same): \fB"8M"\fR.
.SH ERRORS AND COMPATIBILITY
A setting this version does not know, and a transport it does not know, are reported as
warnings with the file and line, and ignored: a file written for a newer version of qsh
works with an older one.
.PP
Any other problem stops qsh with a message that names the file and line: a syntax error, a
value of the wrong type or out of range, a file that is not a regular file, is larger than
1 MiB or is not UTF\-8.
.PP
Like
.BR ssh (1)
with its own files, qsh refuses a file that belongs to a user other than the one running it
or root, or that its group or others may write to, since the file names programs that qsh
runs.
.B chmod go\-w
.I file
fixes the latter. A file that exists but cannot be read is ignored with a warning.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QSH_TRANSPORTS
Comma-separated transports, as
.BR transports ;
overrides the files. For diagnosing networks.
.TP
.B QSH_SSH
The ssh program, as
.BR ssh ;
overrides the files.
.TP
.B QSH_SERVER_PORTS
The port range
.RI ( FIRST\-LAST )
of a
.BR qsh\-server (1)
daemon, as
.B ports
in
.BR [server] ;
overrides the files.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the user's file is:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qsh/config ,
with
.I ~/.config
when unset.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qsh/config
.RI ( ~/.config/qsh/config )
The user's configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/qsh/qsh_config
The system's configuration file.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/qsh/
.RI ( ~/.local/state/qsh/ )
The daemon's certificate and key
.RI ( daemon/ ),
and the log of a daemon started on demand
.RI ( daemon.log ).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/qsh/
.RI ( /tmp/qsh\- UID
when unset)
The daemon's control socket and lock; mode 0700, owned by the user.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# ~/.config/qsh/config

# The build machines are behind a firewall that drops UDP and TCP: use the ssh pipe
[host."build*"]
transports = ["ssh"]

# Hosts of the company, except its lab: no QUIC, no escapes, qsh\-server in /opt
[host."*.corp.example.com,!*.lab.corp.example.com"]
transports = ["tls", "ssh"]
escape_char = "none"
server_command = "/opt/qsh/bin/qsh\-server"

# Everywhere else
[defaults]
escape_char = "^]"
keepalive = 25
ssh_options = ["\-o", "ServerAliveInterval=30"]

# The daemon that qsh\-server starts on this machine
[server]
ports = "61000\-61099"
detached_ttl = "1d"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qsh (1),
.BR qsh\-server (1),
.BR ssh_config (5)
